1
00:00:03,066 --> 00:00:06,036
A topology is the shape of a network.

2
00:00:06,036 --> 00:00:12,781
Bus: every computer taps one backbone cable; cheap, but one break stops everything.

3
00:00:12,781 --> 00:00:21,336
Star: everything connects to a central switch; a cable fault affects one computer, but the switch is a single point of failure.

4
00:00:21,336 --> 00:00:28,393
Ring: data passes round in one direction; equal access, but one break stops the ring.

5
00:00:28,393 --> 00:00:33,719
Mesh: every pair is linked; the most reliable and the most expensive.

6
00:00:33,719 --> 00:00:36,479
Tree: stars arranged in a hierarchy.

7
00:00:38,633 --> 00:00:39,593
The cost of a mesh.

8
00:00:39,866 --> 00:00:47,051
Every pair needs a link: eight times seven over two, twenty eight cables, and seven ports on each computer.

9
00:00:47,266 --> 00:00:49,483
A star needs just one cable per computer.

10
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:53,764
Three and a half times the cable, and the gap grows with n.

11
00:00:53,764 --> 00:00:57,616
That is why mesh is used only where reliability is worth it.

12
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:15,832
The OSI reference model, published by ISO in nineteen eighty four, splits communication into seven layers, each with one job, so any layer can change without disturbing the rest.

13
00:01:15,832 --> 00:01:22,876
Application, presentation, session, transport, network, data link and physical.

14
00:01:22,876 --> 00:01:25,257
All People Seem To Need Data Processing.

15
00:01:25,466 --> 00:01:30,949
As data goes down the stack at the sender, each layer wraps it in its own header.

16
00:01:30,949 --> 00:01:34,283
Transport adds port numbers to make a segment.

17
00:01:34,283 --> 00:01:37,929
Network adds IP addresses to make a packet.

18
00:01:37,929 --> 00:01:42,369
Data link adds MAC addresses and a checksum to make a frame.

19
00:01:42,369 --> 00:01:45,077
The physical layer sends it as bits.

20
00:01:45,077 --> 00:01:49,231
At the receiver, each layer strips its header off on the way up.

21
00:01:49,231 --> 00:01:51,332
This wrapping is called encapsulation.

22
00:01:52,533 --> 00:01:55,022
Each layer's job and protocols.

23
00:01:55,022 --> 00:02:04,385
The Internet itself runs on the simpler TCP IP model, with four layers: the top three OSI layers are merged into one application layer.

24
00:02:09,166 --> 00:02:17,019
An IPv4 address is thirty two bits, written as four numbers from nought to two five five: dotted decimal.

25
00:02:17,019 --> 00:02:21,407
One nine two dot one six eight dot ten dot thirty seven.

26
00:02:21,407 --> 00:02:25,272
In binary, each number is eight bits, an octet.

27
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:29,968
The subnet mask says which bits name the network and which name the host.

28
00:02:29,968 --> 00:02:36,908
Two five five dot two five five dot two five five dot nought: the first twenty four bits are network.

29
00:02:36,908 --> 00:02:42,676
Set the host bits to nought for the network address, to all ones for the broadcast address.

30
00:02:42,676 --> 00:02:48,060
That leaves two to the eight minus two, two hundred and fifty four usable hosts.

31
00:02:49,266 --> 00:02:53,317
Addresses were once grouped into classes by their first octet.

32
00:02:53,317 --> 00:02:57,327
Class A, for huge networks, up to one two six.

33
00:02:57,327 --> 00:03:00,921
Class B, one two eight to one nine one.

34
00:03:00,921 --> 00:03:07,119
Class C, one nine two to two two three, with two hundred and fifty four hosts each.

35
00:03:07,119 --> 00:03:10,674
D is for multicast and E is reserved.

36
00:03:10,674 --> 00:03:21,530
One two seven is loopback, meaning this computer, and three private ranges are reused inside every school and home, sharing one public address through NAT.

37
00:03:22,733 --> 00:03:25,755
Subnetting splits one network into several.

38
00:03:25,755 --> 00:03:30,977
To make four subnets, borrow two host bits, since two squared is four.

39
00:03:30,977 --> 00:03:39,870
The prefix becomes slash twenty six, mask two five five dot two five five dot two five five dot one nine two.

40
00:03:39,870 --> 00:03:49,375
Each subnet has sixty four addresses, sixty two usable, starting at nought, sixty four, one twenty eight and one ninety two.

41
00:03:49,375 --> 00:03:55,079
The second runs from sixty four, the network, to one twenty seven, the broadcast.

42
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:58,372
And keep two addresses apart.

43
00:03:58,372 --> 00:04:05,026
The MAC address is burned into the network card and never changes; switches use it inside one network.

44
00:04:05,026 --> 00:04:12,017
The IP address is logical and changes when you move to a new network; routers use it to cross networks.

45
00:04:14,433 --> 00:04:15,203
Pause and think.

46
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:23,504
A switch.

47
00:04:23,504 --> 00:04:26,342
A hub copies every frame to every port.

48
00:04:28,333 --> 00:04:28,768
One more.

49
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:43,741
Sixty two: six host bits give sixty four addresses, minus the network and broadcast addresses.

50
00:04:45,933 --> 00:04:47,015
Quick recap.

51
00:04:47,015 --> 00:04:50,883
The five elements and three transmission modes.

52
00:04:50,883 --> 00:04:53,122
Guided and unguided media.

53
00:04:53,122 --> 00:04:54,372
The impairments.

54
00:04:54,372 --> 00:04:57,849
Hubs, switches and routers, and the topologies.

55
00:04:57,849 --> 00:05:02,172
The seven OSI layers, wrapping data in headers.

56
00:05:02,172 --> 00:05:05,367
And IP addresses, masks and subnetting.

57
00:05:09,566 --> 00:05:12,107
That was data communication and networking.

58
00:05:12,107 --> 00:05:19,581
Next, web technology: building pages with HTML, CSS, JavaScript and PHP.

59
00:05:19,581 --> 00:05:20,127
See you there.

